{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Lake Vostok Accretion Ice: Subglacial Relics 4km Down",
    "date": "2026-09-15",
    "lesson": "Lake Vostok lies buried under nearly 4km of Antarctic ice, sealed off from the surface for perhaps 15-25 million years. The accretion ice that freezes onto the base of the ice sheet from lake water traps a sparse, cold-adapted microbial community: chemoheterotrophs surviving on trace organic carbon released as ancient lake water refreezes, at temperatures around -3C and crushing pressure with essentially no light. Cell densities are astonishingly low compared to surface seas, division is glacial, and energy budgets are razor-thin. This level asks: can a lineage sustain itself on almost nothing, for a very long time, in total dark, in a sealed world with no allochthonous input beyond what the freezing front itself squeezes out of the water?",
    "citation": "Christner et al. 2006 PNAS; Bulat 2016 Phil Trans R Soc B (Lake Vostok accretion ice microbiology)",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Lake Vostok subglacial lake, East Antarctica, accretion ice zone ~3750m depth"
  },
